{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/643006-an-analysis-on-satiety-and-feeding-hungers","id":643006,"title":"An Analysis on Satiety and Feeding Hungers","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Beast: The Primordial"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-08-28T08:27:18-07:00","id":643006,"title":"An Analysis on Satiety and Feeding Hungers","content":"Satiety. At first glance, it appears to be just another fuel statistic, just like vitae or essence. The truth is there is so much more to Satiety than you would think at first blush. It has an effect on almost every mechanic in the game - from opening Primordial Pathways, to the effectiveness of Atavisms and Nightmares, to your Lair, to abilities like Passing Resemblence and Mother's Kiss. The level of Satiety also comes with one of five Conditions, which has a vast impact on how your character thinks and acts. Just like many other Integrity equivalents, there's also an inverse relationship with the Power Stat. The larger your Lair grows, the harder it is to maintain Satiety levels.<br /> <br /> Satiety also has an effect on Hunger, and how easy it is to feed. At low levels of Satiety, its remarkably easy, but becomes progressively more difficult as your Satiety levels rise. This is known as Satiety Potential. When your Satiety is Low (1-3 points), it just takes an action or so to feed. At Medium Satiety (4-6 points), it requires a bit more planning and looking, requiring an entire scene (roughly an hour of in game time) to achieve. Meanwhile, High Satiety (7-9 points) requires the entire session to seek out and feed. Naturally, you can always feed above your Satiety Potential, which grants you extra dice when rolling to see how much Satiety you gain.<br /> <br /> Another curious way to look at how Satiety and behavior are intertwined is from the five questions to determine starting Satiety levels. If you have Kinship with other supernaturals, gain a point. If you don't grow your Lair, gain a point. If you are careful with your abilities or feeding, gain a point. If you work with friends, gain a point. This shows us a dynamic that is only encouraged throughout the entire game. Low Satiety beasts tend to be more reckless / flashy loners with expansive lair, while the more High Satiety beasts tend to be more careful and have an expansive number of friends and allies as opposed to large Lairs. In fact, its quite interesting to note that the questions are set up such that if you prefer being a more obvious monster with Atavisms and feeding your Hunger, you'll naturally start with lower Satiety, while a character more interested in exploring other supernatural agencies tend to have higher Satiety levels. Low is the Horror focused, High is Kin focused.<br /> <br /> The Integrity-like effects of Satiety manifest in the form of the five Conditions - Ravaging, Starving, Sated, Gorged, and Slumbering. I would like to look at each Condition, how it affects your feeding, ways to feed it, and the effects it has on your actions and abilities in game. Satiety also serves as a bonus to Thicker Than Water (feral only), Passing Resemblence and the Advanced Giant merit as well as a dice pool for Mother's Kiss, Under the Bed, and<br /> <br /> <u><b>Ravaging / Zero Satiety</b></u><br /> It takes some rather extreme situations to reach zero Satiety. I really think that the best way to sum up this Condition? <i>You're dying.</i> Your Horror is lashing out of control, you can't heal - in fact, you're taking damage as time goes on - and you can't even feed your Satiety without a huge meal. You can't even regain willpower in the normal way - sleep and fulfilling your Legend and Life are meaningless, though low potential Hunger feeds can restore willpower points as well as giving you a beat.<br /> <br /> There are two reasons to hit Ravaging - you needed that last boost of power to survive an immediate combat, or you're trying to strip off an Anathem. Now, with removing an Anathem, if you have care, and help of your allies, you can plan a way to hit Ravaging (effectively purging your Horror's system) and immediately feed on something so that you'll be safe. It'll take time and set up, but possible. If its the result of combat, and you're already injured? Even if you won, you're still dying, and there's a very good chance that you'll still die without help. Your wounds only get worse with time, and you don't have access to a whole chunk of your power - no Nightmares work, and you can only access the Normal aspects of your Atavisms - despite being lower than Low, you do not get the Low Satiety benefits.<br /> <br /> Taken together, what happens is that a Ravaging Begotten is going to be committing a string of small Hunger feeds (to replenish willpower and beats) while desperately trying to engage in a dangerous Satiety Potential 8 feeding over the course of an entire session (and likely using all those willpower from the small feedings to do it). If successful, you can survive and feed again normally. If not? Well, hope you have access to some third party with magical healing. Otherwise, good bye and here's a new character sheet. Like Demon: the Descent, its not Game Over if you hit Integrity-equivalent 0, but if you don't fix it fast, it will be.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Starving / Low Satiety</u></b><br /> I really don't like the name &quot;starving&quot; for this level. The character is definitely hungry, but starving implies dying, and that's not what happens here. Though, if you go to long without feeding up to Satiety 4, the Horror will start eating on its own, which can cause all kinds of complications. That said, nothing is stopping a beast from feeding up to Sated, then immediately spending a point of Satiety on something frivalous to reach Low again. In fact, players are encouraged to do just that (covered Sated below).<br /> <br /> The Starving Condition has several effects. First and foremost, a Beast at Low Satiety suffers a -2 penalty to open Primordial Pathways, but she gets to add his Lair dots to the Horror's Power when they merge (this includes opening the Primordial Pathway). This penalty encourages the beast to focus on areas that he's used to create the Lair Chamber he's invoking in the first place and &quot;haunt&quot; those areas as his personal territory - being in this spot also lets the Beast use willpower to open the Path and save the already-low Satiety points for combat. The beast can also invest in Safe Place to become even more at home in these spots. As you can see, Low Satiety also encourages a high Lair score because of the nature of Atavisms - many Low Satiety abilities use the size of the Lair as a bonus.<br /> <br /> Hungers are obscenely easy enough to achieve, that one can actually ignore role playing the feeding and move right to just making a roll off screne, should the plot pacing require it. <br /> <br /> If a character persues a chance to feed their Hunger recklessly, she gains a beat. If the character also hurts a friend or loved one in pursuit of Hunger, gain a beat. When combined with the &quot;reach Satiety 4+&quot; time limit, the Condition encourages reckless behavior, constantly seeking out low level Hunger feeds, and frivalously wasting power on Atavisms for getting whatever you want. This makes feeding your Hunger a rush, akin to an adrenaline junkie.<br /> <br /> The last notable aspect of having Low Satiety is the Anathema. Generally speaking, Anathema at Low Satiety are rather weak and highly managable. Even if a beast is cursed with one, at Low Satiety it hardly impedes her progress in the slightest. As much as its an irritant, the Horror is just too strong at this level to really care that much about it. Can't shrug it off, but it won't fall to it either.<br /> <br /> Bringing all of it together, Low Satiety beasts are the flashy, more obvious monsters constantly &quot;shocking&quot; others to feed their Hunger. They're the greedier, angrier and more ambitious ones, all highly active and driven in ways great and small. Like most adrenaline junkies, the Children are highly physically fit - the Atavisms the Low Satiety Begotten focus on tend to make them physically and socially imposing, personally very powerful, and unafraid of any Heroes or their tricks.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Sated / Medium Satiety</b></u><br /> The Sated Condition is described as being highly uncomfortable, to the point of encouraging a character to quickly feed more or to frivalously spend Satiety in exchange for a beat. Between the vulnerability to a hero's Anathema, the lack of bonuses that both High and Low Satiety brings, and encouragement to either spend or gain more Satiety, the beast has little reason to remain at this point.<br /> <br /> Hungers at this point are still easy to feed. While its not the casual use and immediate gratification that Low Satiety gains, its still only the course of a scene to set up and do something that captures your fancy. We've started moving into the point where, while its not overly difficult yet, its still an effort to feed through your Hunger. On the other hand, Family Dinner is still very easy (see below for more). This is the point where the divide between Low and High Satiety focused beasts becomes more evident. Hunger feeding is easy and appropriate for those Children who maintain Low Satiety, while the High Satiety Begotten focus on Family Dinner. You can still go either way, but its easy to get frustrated at this stage.<br /> <br /> That is not to say that no one will enter Sated (Low Satiety needs to hit Medium every once in a while, and sometimes High Satiety can't feed in time to maintain their level), or that someone won't balance. Like WtF's Harmony score, Satiety is meant to fluxuate between numbers over the course of the game. Unlike Harmony, however, Satiety is not safely balanced at 5; that's the worst spot. Characters are drawn to the extremes, and move to the middle by necessity. That means that a PC is likely to spend most of their time either focusing on Low Satiety, Lair, and Atavisms, or High Satiety, Kinship, and Nightmares. I've only seen one person try to balance the two - spending most of their time at High Satiety, then, when necessary, spending all their Satiety to power a small group of Atavisms for a powerful burst of energy in some situation. There's no restriction on spending Satiety per turn, so you can easily go from High to Ravenous reflexively, so long as you have enough abilities to spend it on. The character also used Fist of Nightmares (sometines with a social power and a handshake) before spending the Satiety. So, balancing is possible, but it won't be a character that spends most of their time in Sated.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Gorged / High Satiety</u></b><br /> A curious level. Unlike the other levels, a Gored Begotten doesn't act much like the primordial monster many people expect. Mechanically, a Gorged character doesn't get her Lair as a bonus to resist any mind-affecting powers, Anathema are at their worst, and you gain beats by failing rolls due to a lazy, sloth like attitude or by being affected by said mind-affecting powers. Atavisms are weaker, while psychic ability-like Nightmares are stronger. Its easier to access Primordial Pathways (+2 bonus) and possibly hitting an exceptional success on them.<br /> <br /> Hungers are nearly impossible to feed at this stage. It requires a full gaming session devoted to just setting up a single feed, for one character. While that's dramatic and appropriate for the full party if one character was Ravenous, constantly devoting entire gaming days to feeding one character detracts from stories. That's where Family Dinner and Kinship comes into play. <br /> <br /> Overall, there are a <i>lot </i>of Kinship benefits at this level of Satiety. You get a higher social bonus when dealing with feral supernaturals, you can appear more like a friend to put them at ease with Passing Resemblence, you can grant Mother's Kisses with far greater ease. The beast at this level are most proficent in Nightmares, and the Begotten will seek out other supernaturals to learn Kinship Nightmares as well as engaging in Family Dinner. High Satiety shoves you full throttle into engaging with other supernaturals and making &quot;friends&quot; through beats (weakness to mind powers), Nightmares, and more. Its almost a kind of curious kind of interaction here - the Begotten will want to use Nightmares as Hard Leverage (opens extra doors at High Satiety), while the opposing supernatural will be bending them to will with their own powers. <br /> <br /> This makes the character an almost human-like mystic at this stage. Evoking environmental magic through Primordial Pathways opening, skill at psychic abilities, physically weaker, and searching out other supernaturals to play with? The laziness and weaker body also encouraged a focus on social connections - getting people to do things for you, instead of yourself. Which ties back again into Family Dinner - why go out and actively feed your own Hunger when being a passive voyeur is just as effective, if not more so?<br /> <br /> I made my players purchase Allies/Contacts/Mentor/Retainer/etc merits to reflect their supernatural connections, which made a curious analog to buying up Lair at low Satiety. Allies at High, Lair at Low. This made calling upon the Kin for various reasons a bit more mchanically consistant, as well as paved ways for the other supernatural to drag the Begotten into their own plots and stories. Yay plot hooks!<br /> <br /> <b><u>Slumbering / Max Satiety</u></b><br /> There's not much to say here. You overfed, either accidentily or on purpose, and now you're effectively mortal, without access to any of your supernatural abilities. If you reached this point by accidentily indulging your Hunger (just -why- didn't you use Family Diner? You can't overindulge then, its safer) too much, then chances are that you've done something to get a Hero on your tail, and hope he can't break into your Lair to slay the slumbering Horror or kill you in the flesh. <br /> <br /> Good if you want to stop being one of the Children and live as an averge human, or shed an Anathema (easy enough to wake up as well, especially if you've got a brood you trust). Not much else to talk about.<br /> <br /> <u><b>Conclusion</b></u><br /> To wrap things up, its basically like there are three ways to play the game - at High, at Low, or a blend of the two. Each has its own attitude, playstyle, and methodology to play with. If you're looking to be an obvious monster that focuses on scaring people and feeding your Hunger, then you will want to play at Low Satiety primarily. There's no reason to raise your Satiety to high levels, and every reason to not. A lot of people are confusing the game, thinking that you should be acting like a Low Satiety beast at all levels of Satiety. That's not quite how it works - the game includes more than one style of play for those interested in exploring different aspects of the World of Darkness. Which is a good thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 08-28-2015, 12:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T10:05:21-07:00","id":643149,"title":"","content":"Pretty thorough analysis, MCN. Nicely pointed out how the mechanics support various playstyles naturally attached to different levels of Satiety and how all of them are valid and have alternatives if something happens that would disturb the desired state achieved by the character.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T10:41:14-07:00","id":643171,"title":"","content":"One question:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/643006-an-analysis-on-satiety-and-feeding-hungers#post643006\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">[When ravenous] you can only access the Normal aspects of your Atavisms - despite being lower than Low, you do not get the Low Satiety benefits.</div> </div> </div> </div>Where did you get this idea from? I can't find anything that even remotely suggests it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T10:56:09-07:00","id":643182,"title":"","content":"Chapter 3, under the Satiety seciton. It defines the five levels of Satiety - Zero Satiety is specifically a different category than Low Satiety, and Low Satiety specifically notes that Atavisms are more effective at that level. Its not the only time when something strange happens; at High Satiety, the character can't add Lair in to resist supernatural mind effects, but at Maximum Satiety, you suddenly can again.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 08-28-2015, 01:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T11:04:15-07:00","id":643187,"title":"","content":"I like it! This shed some new light onto the game for me. By the way, I read before that Heroes could have an Anathema that makes Beasts accidentally reach Satiety 10 when they Feed. Kinda like how <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yamata_no_Orochi\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Susanoo defeated Orochi</b></a> by first making the dragon dip each of its 8 heads into a vat of liquor and drink itself into a slumber. However, this Anathema probably doesn't involve offering actual food and drink to the Beast. It might involve some kind of probability manipulation and hypnotic suggestion to make people gather in a way that is tempting to Feed upon for a Beast with a specific Hunger.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Bye.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","name":"GibberingEloquence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4443_5.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T12:08:14-07:00","id":643242,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/643006-an-analysis-on-satiety-and-feeding-hungers?p=643182#post643182\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Chapter 3, under the Satiety seciton. It defines the five levels of Satiety - Zero Satiety is specifically a different category than Low Satiety, and Low Satiety specifically notes that Atavisms are more effective at that level. Its not the only time when something strange happens; at High Satiety, the character can't add Lair in to resist supernatural mind effects, but at Maximum Satiety, you suddenly can again.</div> </div> </div> </div>The atavism section itself just says &quot;3 or below&quot; though. So I think it got lumped in. Also you can't add lair at max satiety. You're &quot;effectively human&quot; at that level. That precludes supernatural tolerance. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T12:21:53-07:00","id":643251,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/643006-an-analysis-on-satiety-and-feeding-hungers?p=643242#post643242\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The atavism section itself just says &quot;3 or below&quot; though. So I think it got lumped in. Also you can't add lair at max satiety. You're &quot;effectively human&quot; at that level. That precludes supernatural tolerance.</div> </div> </div> </div> I'm aware of the Atavism section, but disagree with your interpretation. I believe its more likely that the Atavism section is a slip, rather than a hard rule, given the causal language. One of the two sections must be wrong, and I find that it makes more sense for Ravaging to not get the bonus from Low Satiety Atavisms - you are literally starving to death, to the point your body is shutting down, and you're supposed to be burning through willpower in a desperate bid of saving your life. Having enhanced physical abilities when you are dying doesn't quite make sense to me, so I'm going with what the official ranking says.<br /> <br /> And, yes, you can use the Tolerance - there's actually no where it says you can't. The Slumbering condition specifically says the following: &quot;She loses access to all her Atavisms, Nightmares, Beast-specific Merits, and Birthrights. She does not lose access to Kinship abilities, but cannot spend Satiety to power them. She does not gain an Integrity trait but is otherwise human. She cannot spend Satiety for any reason and does not naturally lose Satiety, no matter how many dots of Lair she has.&quot; It lists everything that the Slumbering beast loses, and allows Kinship abilities, such as Mother's Kiss, to remain. It says nothing about supernatural tolerance. Now, nothing <i>wrong</i> with running things that way, but its not actually how the rule is worded. <br /> <br /> Frankly, though, Ravaging and Slumbering are minor parts of the game, ones I don't expect an actual PC to be involved in for long with the aid of their brood. Do whatever makes the most sense for your game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T12:54:52-07:00","id":643294,"title":"","content":"The justification for <i>why </i>gorged beast loses their Lair trait as resistance is that their horror is sluggish and unresponsive. In a Slumbering beast it's completely comatose, so having the resistance suddenly switch back on makes <i>no fucking sense whatsoever. </i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}